"""Hillier (1994) Fig. 5 reference curves, for plotting only.

Digitised from Bulla, Sim & Kromer (2015) Fig. 5 (arXiv:1503.07002v1, page 7) at
400 dpi.  The panel axis frame gives the calibration (p = +2 at row 380, p = -4
at row 1267.5, log10 chi0 = -2 at column 454, 0 at column 1600.5); the
independently drawn p = 0 rule lands at row 676, which this calibration maps to
p = -0.001, confirming it.  Each curve is isolated by colour (green 22.5, blue
45, red 67.5, black 90); plotted symbols are rejected by stroke thickness -- they
are Bulla's Monte Carlo points, not Hillier's lines -- and the stroke is followed
with a minimum-total-variation path so the dashed and dash-dot styles survive
their gaps.

These carry read-off error of roughly +-0.05 in p on top of whatever the original
figure carries.  They are a VISUAL REFERENCE ONLY.  The regression gate is the
analytic thin limit computed in main.cpp, which needs no external data; nothing
here is ever a pass/fail criterion.

Keys are inclination in degrees; values are (log10 chi0, p percent) pairs.
"""

# Four distinct hues, ordered cool -> warm with increasing inclination.  A single
# blue hue separates the series only by lightness, which collapses as soon as the
# curves cross -- and these cross twice, at the turnover and at the sign reversal.
# Hue carries the identity; the cool-to-warm order keeps inclination readable as
# an ordered quantity rather than four unrelated categories.
RAMP = ["#3c78d8", "#16a085", "#e67e22", "#c0392b"]
# Same hues lifted for dark backgrounds, where the deeper red and blue close up.
RAMP_DARK = ["#6fa4ea", "#2ecc9f", "#f0a05a", "#e8635a"]

